TI Mapping the multidimensional structure-property landscape of polyolefin elastomers with interpretable machine learning
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
DE Polyolefin elastomer; Materials genome; Machine learning; Structure-property relationship; Data-driven molecular design
ID MATERIALS DISCOVERY; ALPHA-OLEFINS; COPOLYMERIZATION; ETHYLENE; CATALYSTS; HOMOPOLYMERIZATION; ETHYLENE/1-OCTENE; 1-HEXENE; 1-DECENE; DESIGN
AB Rational design of polyolefin elastomers (POEs) is hindered by their intrinsic black box nature, which stems from complex, nonlinear relationships between their molecular architecture (e.g., comonomer sequence, composition, and chain topology) and macroscopic performance. This study addresses this challenge by integrating a curated materials genome from synthesis with interpretable machine learning. A rigorously benchmarked ensemble model based on the Gradient Boosting Decision Tree algorithm demonstrates exceptional accuracy (R-2 of 0.852 similar to 0.943) in predicting seven key thermal, mechanical, and processing properties. SHAP interpretability analysis is applied to transform the model into a quantitative, correlative framework for descriptor mining, enabling systematic mining of key descriptors. Specific triadic sequence distributions and molecular parameters are identified as key regulatory factors. Triadic sequence distribution exhibits pronounced contributions to density, melting point (T-m), melt flow rate (MFR), and elongation at break (EAB), often surpassing the influence of comonomer content emphasized in prior studies, often surpassing the traditional focus on comonomer content. Molecular weight (M-w) is quantitatively identified as the foremost positive contributor to tensile strength (TS) and a secondary negative driver of T-m, while positively regulating crystallinity (X-c) and glass transition temperature (T-g). 1-Octene content (C1-octene) acts as a prevalent negative contributor to density, X-c, T-g, EAB, and TS. This study demonstrates that POE performance arises not from a single parameter but from concerted interplay ofC(1-octene), triadic sequence distribution, and M-w, with sequence architecture often exerting decisive influence beyond the traditional focus onC(1-octene). These descriptors form a foundational blueprint, advancing phenomenological correlation to statistically informed structure-property insights and providing precise, interpretable rules for rational, performance-targeted molecular design of advanced POEs.
