TI Computational approaches to optimize polymerization and curing in low-temperature thermosetting composites for enhanced additive manufacturing efficiency
SO THERMAL SCIENCE AND ENGINEERING PROGRESS
LA English
DT Article
DE Thermosetting composites; Process optimisation; Additive manufacturing; Machine learning; Cure kinetics; Computational modelling
ID MATRIX COMPOSITES
AB This work offers a new holistic computational model for the polymerisation and curing simulation of lowtemperature thermosetting polymer materials fabricated by AM. Based on the systematic approach that combined machine learning (ML), finite element analysis (FEA), and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations, we have formulated an efficient strategy to overcome the non-uniform curing process, extended processing time, and quality variations of composite manufacturing. The framework integrates a DNN model for the prognosis of the process parameters, an FEA model with accuracy for thermal mechanical analysis, and an MD analysis for atomistic information. The implementation resulted in significant improvements: The findings of this work include a decrease in cure time by 45\%, respectively 240+15 to 132+8 min, increased degree of cure equal to 97.2+0.3, and void content equal to 0.8+0.1. It was possible to improve the accuracy of process control to within +1 degrees C and pressure variation within +0.1 bar. Analysis of process data showed strong process capability, Cp = 1.85 and a high process yield of 99.7\%, while using computational predictions had a prediction accuracy of 97\ %. The prediction of all developed methods in each of these directions exposed the supremacy of the hybrid integrated optimisation framework over the standard approach in terms of energy consumption (2.4+0.1 kWh/ kg) and defect rate (0.3\%). These changes were accompanied by experimental characterisation of the composite material and microstructure analysis to ensure that they were indeed beneficial. The improvement of the manufacturing process of composite structures is the contribution of this work in the knowledge advancement of composite manufacturing technology by providing an efficient computation model with high experimental validity that augments production rates while inflicting no quality detriment. The conclusion of this study is profoundly relevant to the large-scale production of high-performance composite structures.
