PT J
AU Riaz, MH
   Sani, SFA
   Hasnan, HK
   Manan, NSA
AF Riaz, Muhammad Hammad
   Sani, S. F. Abdul
   Hasnan, Hijaz Kamal
   Manan, Ninie Suhana Abdul
TI The role of rare earth elements and hybrid fillers based polymer composites for space radiation shielding
SO PHYSICS REPORTS-REVIEW SECTION OF PHYSICS LETTERS
LA English
DT Review
DE Space radiation shielding; Polymer composites; Hydrogen-rich polymers; Rare-earth oxides; High-Z fillers; Neutron-gamma attenuation; Metal-matrix composites; Radiation transport simulation; Machine-learning-assisted design; Space environment degradation; Areal density optimization
ID ATOMIC OXYGEN; NANOCOMPOSITE MATERIALS; MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; NEUTRON; DESIGN; POLYETHYLENE; MICROSTRUCTURE; ENVIRONMENT; PROTECTION; APPLICABILITY
AB Space missions are constrained by a complex mixed-radiation environment, dominated by galactic cosmic rays, solar energetic particles, and trapped radiation, which impose cumulative risks to spacecraft electronics and long-duration human habitats. Conventional aluminum-based structures, although widely used, provide limited intrinsic attenuation and often contribute to the generation of secondary radiation under high-energy irradiation. This review critically examines recent progress in hybrid polymer-based radiation-shielding materials to address these limitations. Metal-matrix composites with space heritage are initially evaluated, highlighting inherent constraints related to mass penalties and secondary radiation production. Polymer-based composites are examined, with emphasis on hydrogen-rich matrices integrated with rare-earth-oxide and high-Z fillers that enable synergistic proton moderation, neutron capture, and gamma attenuation within mass-constrained architectures. Radiation-matter interaction mechanisms, including proton energy loss, nuclear fragmentation and secondary particle generation, photon attenuation processes, and neutron moderation-capture processes, are linked to materials selection and composite design. Beyond shielding performance, space-environment durability is evaluated under synergistic stressors including ionizing radiation, UV-atomic oxygen erosion, thermal cycling, and vacuum outgassing. Mission-relevant case studies, including AstroRad Vest, CubeSat hybrid shielding, and MISSE-validated polymers, demonstrate the transition of laboratory materials into flight-ready solutions. Finally, the integration of simulation tools with data-centric materials design and machine-learning-assisted optimization is highlighted as a transformative pathway for conceiving mission-adaptive shielding architectures for future deep-space exploration. Persistent challenges such as secondary radiation control, particle dispersion, long-term degradation, and the lack of standardized mixed field testing are identified as important priorities for next-generation lightweight and reliable shielding systems. (c) 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
