TI Synergistic tuning of ionic conductivity and Young's modulus of bio-based polymers by integrating molecular dynamics simulation and machine learning
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
AB Developing polymer electrolytes that possess both high ionic conductivity and excellent mechanical strength remains a key challenge in the field of energy storage. By addressing the dilemma that these two macroscopic properties often mutually restrict each other and are inherently decoupled, this study innovatively proposes a small-sample, multi-output, data-driven computational framework combining molecular dynamics (MD) simulation and machine learning (ML) for the design and optimization of bio-based ferulic acid-derived ion-conducting polymers. By constructing a high-quality MD dataset, we systematically evaluated six ML algorithms and found that Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) exhibited the optimal predictive accuracy and robustness when handling complex nonlinear structure-property mappings. A significant innovation of this research lies in its multi-output architecture design, which successfully integrates the learning processes of ion transport and mechanical strength (two decoupled attributes), achieving efficient joint prediction without sacrificing the predictive accuracy of either individual target. Combined with SHAP interpretability analysis, we extracted the key microscopic features determining material performance. This not only clarified the absolutely dominant role of lithium salt concentration in both properties but also distilled a rational design guideline for polymer electrolytes by balancing molecular skeletal rigidity, cohesive energy, and segmental flexibility. Relying on this high-precision GPR model, high-throughput screening of 9240 virtual formulations was performed, successfully identifying and subsequently verifying via MD simulations an optimal formulation combination that simultaneously delivers high room-temperature conductivity (5.50 & times; 10-4 S/cm) and high Young's modulus (101.37 MPa). This work not only provides a reliable predictive tool for the accelerated research and development of novel bio-based polymer materials, but also offers highly valuable theoretical guidance for breaking the trade-off effect in material performance.
