TI AI-nanomaterial co-design for advanced polymeric pipeline coatings: Corrosion protection mechanisms and intelligent design strategies
SO PROGRESS IN ORGANIC COATINGS
LA English
DT Review
DE Polymeric coatings; Nanocomposite coatings; Corrosion protection; Pipeline coatings; Artificial intelligence; Coating degradation; Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy; Materials informatics
ID ANTICORROSION COATINGS; STEEL; ANODE; FILM; OIL
AB Corrosion-induced degradation of oil and gas pipelines remains a major challenge to infrastructure safety, operational reliability, and long-term service durability, particularly in aggressive environments characterized by chlorides, CO2, H2S, elevated temperatures, and multiphase flow conditions. Conventional polymeric coating systems, although widely employed for corrosion protection, often suffer from limitations in permeability, interfacial degradation, mechanical damage, and environmental aging under long-term exposure. Recent advances in nanomaterial-reinforced polymeric coatings have created new opportunities for enhancing barrier resistance, interfacial adhesion, mechanical robustness, and multifunctional performance through engineered nanoscale interactions and optimized coating architectures. This review critically examines recent progress in nanocomposite coatings for pipeline corrosion mitigation, with emphasis on the structure-property-performance relationships governing anticorrosion behavior. The roles of graphene-based nanomaterials, carbon nanotubes, MXenes, nanosilica, metal oxides, and hybrid nanofillers are discussed in relation to diffusion resistance, crack inhibition, electrochemical stability, adhesion enhancement, and long-term durability under complex service conditions. Recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning are reviewed with respect to coating formulation optimization, corrosion prediction, degradation forecasting, uncertainty-aware materials discovery, and accelerated experimental design. Beyond summarizing recent progress, this review proposes an AI-nanomaterial co-design framework that integrates multiscale materials engineering, physics-guided optimization, multimodal data fusion, and iterative experimental validation within a unified coating development workflow. This framework establishes a closed-loop interaction between computational intelligence and experimental evidence to accelerate coating design while maintaining engineering feasibility and physical consistency. Current challenges associated with nanomaterial dispersion, scalable manufacturing, environmental durability, standardized datasets, trustworthy AI, and field validation are also discussed. Overall, this review provides both a comprehensive synthesis of current advances and a conceptual roadmap toward intelligent, adaptive, and deployable polymeric coating systems for next-generation energy pipeline infrastructure.
