TI Medical detection of osteosarcoma biomarkers through machine learning and electrochemical sensors: the macromolecular mechanism of BAG2 and MGA proteins
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Electrochemical sensor; Osteosarcoma; Apoptosis; Mendelian randomization; Prognostic model; Immune microenvironment; Experimental validation; Drug sensitivity
ID CANCER; PATHWAY; EXPRESSION; APOPTOSIS; LAMINS; CELLS; OX40
AB Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor. Due to delayed diagnosis, limited treatment options and easy drug resistance, the prognosis is extremely poor. There is an urgent need for precise biomarkers and detection technologies to support the optimization of diagnosis and treatment. As a cutting-edge technology in the field of medical testing, electrochemical sensors, with their advantages of high sensitivity, high specificity, rapid response and portability, have irreplaceable value in the trace detection and real-time diagnosis of tumor markers, and can effectively make up for the deficiencies of traditional detection methods in the early screening of osteosarcoma. This study aims to integrate multi-omics data and electrochemical sensor technology to identify biomarkers related to osteosarcoma apoptosis and construct a prognostic model. The study screened candidate genes through the intersection of transcriptome data, cis-eQTL data and apoptosis-related genes, and identified core hub genes by combining Mendelian randomization analysis and three machine learning algorithms: LASSO, SVM and random forest. At the same time, electrochemical sensor technology is introduced. Based on specific antibody modification, nanomaterial signal amplification and multi-channel design, highly sensitive quantitative detection of target proteins is achieved, complementing Western blot for verification. The results showed that four apoptosis-related hub genes, namely BAG2, LMNA, MGA, and PPP4R1, which were significantly causal associated with the risk of osteosarcoma, were successfully screened out. Electrochemical sensor detection confirmed that BAG2, MGA, and PPP4R1 were highly expressed in osteosarcoma tissues and cell lines, while LMNA was lowly expressed. The detection limit reached the picomolar level, and multi-target detection could be completed simultaneously, providing precise data for marker validation. The prognostic model constructed based on these four genes can effectively divide patients into high-risk and low-risk groups. The 1-5 year survival AUC values of both the training set and the validation set are good. The immune infiltration level in the high-risk group decreased, and they were sensitive to PARP inhibitors, etc. The low-risk group responded better to MEK inhibitors.
