TI Diagnostic role of CTLA4 and NT5E macromolecular mechanisms and tumor molecular markers based on electrochemical sensors in MSI and MSS subtypes of endometrial cancer
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Electrochemical sensor; Endometrial carcinoma; Microsatellite instability; T cell exhaustion; Molecular networks; Prognostic model
AB Electrochemical sensors can convert the specific binding of tumor biomolecules into quantifiable electrical signals, enabling in-situ real-time detection of target molecules, thereby compensating for the cumbersome operation, long duration and high cost of traditional immunohistochemistry and gene sequencing in the detection of endometrial cancer. Endometrial cancer is a common malignant tumor of the female reproductive system, and there is significant molecular heterogeneity between the microsatellite unstable (MSI) and microsatellite stable (MSS) subtypes. T cell exhaustion and immune regulatory molecules are core markers for subtype classification, prognosis assessment and immunotherapy decision-making, and there is an urgent need to establish a precise detection system based on electrochemical sensing technology. This study aims to integrate multi-omics machine learning to analyze the T cell exhaustion and immune-related molecular networks of MSI/MSS subtypes in endometrial cancer, screen key diagnostic and prognostic markers, and construct a prognostic risk model, providing target information for precise endometrial cancer testing based on electrochemical sensors. The study is based on multi-omics datasets from TCGA, GTEx, and GEO, screens differentially expressed genes related to T cell exhaustion and immunity, and analyzes the molecular functional pathways through GO, KEGG and GSEA enrichment analysis. Through Cox regression and LASSO regression, a prognostic model is constructed, and hub genes are identified and their regulatory networks and single-cell expression localization are analyzed. The results show that 34 key genes were screened out, mainly enriched in cell adhesion, immune regulation and tumor immune checkpoint pathways; a prognostic risk model containing 10 genes was successfully constructed and verified to have independent prognostic value, with the 5-year prognostic prediction efficacy being the best; finally, 5 hub genes, CTLA4, NT5E, ANXA1, TNFRSF14 and CXCL13, were identified, which show significant differential expression in endometrial cancer and have clear cellular localization characteristics, and the tumors in the high-risk group present an "immune cold" phenotype.
