TI Identification of CCL2, CCL5, and TAGLN macromolecules as diagnostic biomarkers for dilated cardiomyopathy through electrochemical sensor analysis: Aging-Associated Immune Dysregulation and Fibrosis
SO MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Electrochemical sensor; Dilated cardiomyopathy; Aging; Immune biomarkers; Single-cell RNA sequencing; Inflammation; Fibrosis; Diagnostic model
ID TUMOR-SUPPRESSIVE FUNCTION; PROTEIN; RECRUITMENT; MYOCARDITIS; MECHANISMS; TRANSGELIN; STATEMENT; PROMOTES; FAILURE; MIR-1
AB Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) is the main cause of heart failure and cardiogenic sudden death. Aging-related immune dysregulation and myocardial fibrosis are the core pathological processes of DCM. Current clinical diagnostic methods have drawbacks such as invasiveness, delay, and insufficient accuracy. Therefore, there is an urgent need for non-invasive diagnostic technologies and biomarkers that are compatible with modern medical testing. Electrochemical sensors, as a core precise detection technology in the field of medical testing, with high sensitivity, specificity, low sample consumption, rapid quantification, and bedside rapid detection adaptability, have become the key support for the serological detection of large molecule biomarkers of cardiovascular diseases. They can achieve non-invasive quantitative detection of low-abundance inflammatory and fibrotic-related proteins at the pg/mL level. The multi-component sensing array can also complete synchronous detection of multiple biomarkers, perfectly meeting the clinical testing requirements of DCM's aging-related biomarkers. This is an important technical path to solve the problem of early diagnosis of DCM. This study aims to screen DCM aging-related immune-fibrotic biomarkers detectable by electrochemical sensors, construct a highly accurate diagnostic model, and provide theoretical basis for the clinical transformation of DCM testing. The study integrates single-cell RNA sequencing data, animal models, and clinical samples for multi-dimensional analysis. Through bioinformatics, aging activity-related genes are screened, and core diagnostic markers are identified through various machine learning algorithms. A diagnostic model is constructed and validated in independent datasets, clinical, and animal models. The results show that CCL2, CCL5, and TAGLN are core immune-fibrotic biomarkers of DCM, significantly upregulated in cardiac fibroblasts and myeloid cells, and closely related to immune infiltration and myocardial fibrosis. The diagnostic model constructed based on these three biomarkers has an AUC of 0.927, which can effectively distinguish DCM from other cardiomyopathies. The high expression of the biomarkers is associated with poor clinical prognosis of patients. The validation results confirm the excellent stability of the model. These biomarkers are all secreted large molecule proteins that can be efficiently detected by electrochemical sensors and are suitable for non-invasive testing in serum.
