TI Laser ultrasonics for multi-scale evaluation of additive manufacturing components: Focus on polymer-composite systems
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
DE Additive manufacturing; Laser ultrasonics; Nondestructive testing; Defect assessment; Artificial intelligence; Composite materials
ID WAVE ELASTIC-ANISOTROPY; MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; GENERATED ULTRASOUND; PROCESS PARAMETERS; CARBON
AB With the rapid adoption of additive manufacturing (AM) technologies in aerospace and high-end manufacturing, polymer composite materials have become an indispensable class of materials within AM systems due to their low density, high specific strength, and exceptional design flexibility. However, the complex mechanisms of defect formation, the difficulty of defect detection, and insufficient quality traceability remain critical bottlenecks that hinder their broader engineering application. Laser ultrasonics (LU), characterized by its non-contact operation, wide bandwidth, and multimodal capabilities, is widely regarded as one of the most promising techniques for enabling in situ, real-time, and quantitative quality assessment of AM components. The purpose of this paper is to systematically review the development trajectory and key advances of LU in nondestructive evaluation for additive manufacturing, with particular emphasis on its application characteristics and emerging trends in polymer composite components. Within an integrated multiscale framework linking defects, microstructure, and performance, this review aims to elucidate the unique role of LU within existing nondestructive testing methodologies. In contrast to previous reviews that primarily focus on process monitoring or single-signal analysis, this work adopts a novel perspective by integrating mechanism-based modeling, advanced signal processing, and intelligent reconstruction. By coupling the physical principles of LU with data-driven algorithms, the multiscale potential of LU for defect identification, quantitative microstructural characterization, and mechanical property prediction is comprehensively analyzed. This review demonstrates that LU not only overcomes the limitations of conventional ultrasonic testing, X-ray computed tomography, and eddy current inspection in environments involving complex geometries, elevated temperatures, and rough surfaces, but also enables a paradigm shift from geometric defect detection to material behavior sensing through approaches such as dispersion analysis, guided-wave inversion, and deep learning. By addressing the lack of a systematic synthesis of LU-based multilevel applications in additive manufacturing reported in the existing literature, this paper clarifies the central role of LU in the development of intelligent and standardized quality control systems. Overall, this work provides a theoretical foundation for understanding the physical mechanisms and application logic of LU in the AM domain and lays the groundwork for future advances in real-time monitoring, digital twins, and the production of highly reliable components.
