TI Effect of stacking architecture on the low-velocity impact behaviour of carbon fibre reinforced polymer plates: A physics-informed screening framework based on lamination parameters, orientation entropy and an interface mismatch relation
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
DE Carbon fibre reinforced polymer; Low-velocity impact; Stacking sequence; Lamination parameters; Cohesive zone model; Helicoidal laminate; Sparse regression; Bio-inspired composites; Bouligand structure
ID TOUGHENING MECHANISMS; COMPOSITE; SIMULATION; EPOXY; TOUGHNESS; SIZE
AB Low-velocity impact (LVI) damage in carbon fibre reinforced polymer (CFRP) laminated plates depends strongly on the stacking sequence, yet physics-informed frameworks linking the architecture to the impact response, useable before a detailed finite element analysis (FEA), are limited. In this study the 33.5 J LVI behaviour of ten T300/5208 stacking architectures is examined in a combined Hashin intralaminar and cohesive-surface interlaminar Abaqus/Explicit framework with nine cohesive interfaces. A three-threshold delamination classification (CSDMG >0, >0.05, = 1) separates initiation, partial evolution and full debonding. The primary controlling parameter is the in-plane orthotropy |V1A|, which with a secondary descriptor, the normalised Shannon orientation entropy H-norm, represents the peak contact force and the maximum deflection with R-2 = 0.92 and 0.87, and Q(2) = 0.77 and 0.48 in leave-one-out cross-validation (n = 10, design-envelope screening model). The lower Q(2) = 0.48 of the deflection model marks limited predictive power, so within the studied envelope this model is used to rank the architectures rather than to predict absolute deflection, while the peak-force model is the more reliable of the two and rests on |V1A| as its dominant, bootstrap-significant descriptor. Significance is supported by a non-parametric permutation test (p = 0.008). A dimensionless damage performance index identifies only two Pareto-optimal architectures: the quasi-isotropic reference and the helicoidal 15 degrees pitch variant, the latter giving a Pareto-equivalent 2.9 percent smaller full-debonded area. A proposed mid-plane concentration relation strongly explains the mid-plane cohesive interface dominance through the parabolic transverse-shear distribution of classical plate theory: R-2 = 0.88 for the nine interface means (n = 9) and R-2 = 0.29 for all 90 interface-layup points (n = 90). The framework is positioned not as a replacement for data-driven machine learning, but as a mechanically interpretable pre-screening tool. The 2.9 percent smaller full-debonded area of the helicoidal architecture is close to the residual mesh-convergence uncertainty of the delamination area and is therefore reported as a modest rather than a decisive advantage. The coefficients apply to the studied envelope of 40-ply T300/5208 laminates, 5 mm thickness, ASTMD7136geometry and 33.5 J energy.
